;;; A refutation over a relation of the program's own read closed-world
;;; because a module imported for it loads an obligation rule —
;;; regression `connex-import` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `std.algebra` imported for `connex`, over `lt`, a relation the program
;;; declares over three nodes and states two facts of, `(lt A B)` and `(lt B
;;; C)`. It names no obligation and activates none, and nothing closes `lt`;
;;; the import loads `std.algebra`'s two obligation rules with the module.
;;; A and C are not compared, so `connex` concludes `(false)` at root.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No hrule, and no obligation activated at root, so the blind enumerator
;;; builds H, and it proposes `lt`: `(lt B A)`, `(lt C A)`, `(lt C B)` and
;;; `(lt A C)`. `connex` concludes `(false)` from an `absent` over `lt`, a
;;; relation a commitment can still extend, which *forbid* refuses: every
;;; question about a commitment exits 1, naming `connex` and its remedy,
;;; `(solve :no-hypothesis (lt))`; `ein saturate` still saturates.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; It loaded: no candidate, root dead, R empty, and `lt` named outside the
;;; universe, *no obligation owes it*. The loaded obligation rules took the
;;; ladder's second rung, which owed nothing and proposed nothing, so no
;;; commitment extended `lt` and the `absent` was read closed-world. Today
;;; the second rung is taken where an obligation is activated at root, and
;;; the program is refused at load, as above: a program that loaded, and
;;; whose remedy is to say what it means, `(solve :no-hypothesis (lt))`.

(import std.algebra :symbols (connex))
(relation lt Node Node :why "{?1} is less than {?2}")
(instance A Node) (instance B Node) (instance C Node)
(connex lt instance Node)
(lt A B) (lt B C)
(query :goal (lt ?x ?y))
